{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012s3J5nbtgquYhpEu26adVe","type":"tool_result","content":"# Deep Start Review Loop Presentation Contract\n\nPresentation source of truth for `/deep:review`. The command router owns only mode selection, safety gates, and asset loading. Workflow YAML owns execution. This file owns visible prompts, dashboard/checkpoint layout, result displays, examples, and next-step suggestions.\n\n## 1. Startup Presentation\n\nThis workflow gathers all setup inputs in one prompt. Confirm mode still includes multiple approval gates after setup; only the setup round-trip is consolidated here.\n\nFor `:confirm` or no suffix, the consolidated setup prompt MUST be the first visible response. No implementation or file-modifying tool calls before asking. Lightweight read-only discovery to suggest a spec folder or load prior context is allowed, then ask ALL questions immediately and wait.\n\nFor `:auto`, do not emit the consolidated prompt by default. Resolve setup with the three-tier branch below, then load the workflow YAML only after all required values are bound.\n\n### Offer From an Advisor Brief\n\nUse this when the operator did not type the command and one of two things brought you here: a\nbrief or advisor result named `/deep:review` with policy `ask` or with no policy, or the request\nasks for the loop itself. Asking for the loop means it names a deep loop, deep review or a review\nloop, gives a count of two or more iterations or \"one more\", says the review itself runs in\niterations (\"iterative review\", \"audit it iteratively\", \"in several iterations\"), says\nmulti-pass or several passes, or asks to continue, resume or extend a deep run. A bare\n\"iterations\" or \"iterative\" that describes the subject is not a loop ask: \"the iterative\nsolver\", \"the first two iterations\" and \"failed after 3 iterations\" name the subject or past\nruns. Depth words alone, such as \"thorough\", \"in depth\", \"exhaustive\" or \"final verdict\", are\nnot a loop ask either: \"do a thorough code review\" stays a single-pass review and gets no\noffer. A missing policy counts as\n`ask`. An attached `:auto` or `:confirm` suffix means the mode is already chosen, so skip the\noffer: that covers the run you start after a yes and any dispatched child. A brief whose policy\nis `run` or `start` follows that policy, not this offer.\n\nAsk once, through the question tool that `.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md` §5\nnames for your runtime, in the same call as the Gate 3 spec folder question. The offer is one\nquestion with exactly three options, so it fits a tool that takes two to four. Put one composed\ninvocation in front of the operator, mark it recommended and say what it spends. Only where §5\nnames no tool for this session, print the offer as text.\n\n  Run a deep review loop on \"<target>\"?\n  A) Run it (Recommended): <composed invocation>\n     Reviews <target type> \"<target>\" across all four dimensions, up to <N> iterations on <executor>. Writes to <spec folder>/review/. <one clause per flag you added and why>\n  B) Step through setup first: /deep:review:confirm \"<target>\"\n     Asks every setup value and stops for approval before each iteration.\n81:0. When no `:auto` or `:confirm` suffix is attached, the operator did not type this command, and either a brief named it with policy `ask` or no policy or the request asks for the loop itself, compose and ask the offer in the presentation contract's \"Offer From an Advisor Brief\" section before anything else. A missing advisor policy counts as `ask`. The offer is one question, asked in the same call as the Gate 3 spec folder question, with three options: A) run the composed invocation with the `:auto` suffix and the Gate 3 answer as its spec folder, marked recommended, B) step through setup with `/deep:review:confirm`, C) no loop, a single-pass review. A composed command shown for approval without these three options is not the offer. After A, run it with the `:auto` suffix, the composed flags and the Gate 3 answer as its spec folder.\n82-1. Parse `$ARGUMENTS` for attached command suffixes (`:auto` or `:confirm`). Canonical mode syntax is `/deep:review:auto` and `/deep:review:confirm`.\n83-2. Treat the target text, pre-bound setup answers and every flag in the Workflow Flag Surface table below as workflow inputs, not execution modes. The table is the only list of flags.\n84-3. If `:auto` is present, set `execution_mode = AUTONOMOUS` and resolve required setup inputs through the presentation contract's three-tier auto setup resolution before loading YAML.\n85-4. If `:confirm` is present, set `execution_mode = INTERACTIVE` and use the presentation contract's consolidated setup prompt before loading YAML.\n86-5. If no mode suffix is present, set `execution_mode = ASK` and use the presentation contract's consolidated setup prompt to ask for execution mode.\n87-6. Load the selected workflow asset only after `review_target`, `review_target_type`, `review_dimensions`, `spec_folder`, `execution_mode`, `maxIterations`, `convergenceThreshold`, `convergence_mode`, `depth_mode`, `stop_policy`, `lineage_mode` and `dry_run` are bound.\n56:A deep-loop command with policy `ask` also names its three offer options, so a session can ask the offer before it opens the command doc: `use system-deep-loop → /deep:review (ask: A /deep:review:auto (Recommended), B /deep:review:confirm, C no loop) 0.95/0.12 pass.` A dispatched child gets `(parent-named only)` instead, with no options. A deep-loop command the prompt typed, with or without a mode suffix, prints plain `(ask)`, because the reader already chose: `use system-deep-loop → /deep:review (ask) 0.94/0.12 pass.`","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"<redacted>","uuid":"<redacted>","timestamp":"2026-11-24T04:52:37.681Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Deep Start Review Loop Presentation Contract\n\nPresentation source of truth for `/deep:review`. The command router owns only mode selection, safety gates, and asset loading. Workflow YAML owns execution. This file owns visible prompts, dashboard/checkpoint layout, result displays, examples, and next-step suggestions.\n\n## 1. Startup Presentation\n\nThis workflow gathers all setup inputs in one prompt. Confirm mode still includes multiple approval gates after setup; only the setup round-trip is consolidated here.\n\nFor `:confirm` or no suffix, the consolidated setup prompt MUST be the first visible response. No implementation or file-modifying tool calls before asking. Lightweight read-only discovery to suggest a spec folder or load prior context is allowed, then ask ALL questions immediately and wait.\n\nFor `:auto`, do not emit the consolidated prompt by default. Resolve setup with the three-tier branch below, then load the workflow YAML only after all required values are bound.\n\n### Offer From an Advisor Brief\n\nUse this when the operator did not type the command and one of two things brought you here: a\nbrief or advisor result named `/deep:review` with policy `ask` or with no policy, or the request\nasks for the loop itself. Asking for the loop means it names a deep loop, deep review or a review\nloop, gives a count of two or more iterations or \"one more\", says the review itself runs in\niterations (\"iterative review\", \"audit it iteratively\", \"in several iterations\"), says\nmulti-pass or several passes, or asks to continue, resume or extend a deep run. A bare\n\"iterations\" or \"iterative\" that describes the subject is not a loop ask: \"the iterative\nsolver\", \"the first two iterations\" and \"failed after 3 iterations\" name the subject or past\nruns. Depth words alone, such as \"thorough\", \"in depth\", \"exhaustive\" or \"final verdict\", are\nnot a loop ask either: \"do a thorough code review\" stays a single-pass review and gets no\noffer. A missing policy counts as\n`ask`. An attached `:auto` or `:confirm` suffix means the mode is already chosen, so skip the\noffer: that covers the run you start after a yes and any dispatched child. A brief whose policy\nis `run` or `start` follows that policy, not this offer.\n\nAsk once, through the question tool that `.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md` §5\nnames for your runtime, in the same call as the Gate 3 spec folder question. The offer is one\nquestion with exactly three options, so it fits a tool that takes two to four. Put one composed\ninvocation in front of the operator, mark it recommended and say what it spends. Only where §5\nnames no tool for this session, print the offer as text.\n\n  Run a deep review loop on \"<target>\"?\n  A) Run it (Recommended): <composed invocation>\n     Reviews <target type> \"<target>\" across all four dimensions, up to <N> iterations on <executor>. Writes to <spec folder>/review/. <one clause per flag you added and why>\n  B) Step through setup first: /deep:review:confirm \"<target>\"\n     Asks every setup value and stops for approval before each iteration.\n81:0. When no `:auto` or `:confirm` suffix is attached, the operator did not type this command, and either a brief named it with policy `ask` or no policy or the request asks for the loop itself, compose and ask the offer in the presentation contract's \"Offer From an Advisor Brief\" section before anything else. A missing advisor policy counts as `ask`. The offer is one question, asked in the same call as the Gate 3 spec folder question, with three options: A) run the composed invocation with the `:auto` suffix and the Gate 3 answer as its spec folder, marked recommended, B) step through setup with `/deep:review:confirm`, C) no loop, a single-pass review. A composed command shown for approval without these three options is not the offer. After A, run it with the `:auto` suffix, the composed flags and the Gate 3 answer as its spec folder.\n82-1. Parse `$ARGUMENTS` for attached command suffixes (`:auto` or `:confirm`). Canonical mode syntax is `/deep:review:auto` and `/deep:review:confirm`.\n83-2. Treat the target text, pre-bound setup answers and every flag in the Workflow Flag Surface table below as workflow inputs, not execution modes. The table is the only list of flags.\n84-3. If `:auto` is present, set `execution_mode = AUTONOMOUS` and resolve required setup inputs through the presentation contract's three-tier auto setup resolution before loading YAML.\n85-4. If `:confirm` is present, set `execution_mode = INTERACTIVE` and use the presentation contract's consolidated setup prompt before loading YAML.\n86-5. If no mode suffix is present, set `execution_mode = ASK` and use the presentation contract's consolidated setup prompt to ask for execution mode.\n87-6. Load the selected workflow asset only after `review_target`, `review_target_type`, `review_dimensions`, `spec_folder`, `execution_mode`, `maxIterations`, `convergenceThreshold`, `convergence_mode`, `depth_mode`, `stop_policy`, `lineage_mode` and `dry_run` are bound.\n56:A deep-loop command with policy `ask` also names its three offer options, so a session can ask the offer before it opens the command doc: `use system-deep-loop → /deep:review (ask: A /deep:review:auto (Recommended), B /deep:review:confirm, C no loop) 0.95/0.12 pass.` A dispatched child gets `(parent-named only)` instead, with no options. A deep-loop command the prompt typed, with or without a mode suffix, prints plain `(ask)`, because the reader already chose: `use system-deep-loop → /deep:review (ask) 0.94/0.12 pass.`","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012s3J5nbtgquYhpEu26adVe","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
